---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import RocksSim from '../../simulators/rocks/RocksSim.astro';
import { rocksQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/rocks';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is the rock cycle?' },
  { id: 'igneous', label: 'Igneous rocks' },
  { id: 'sedimentary', label: 'Sedimentary rocks' },
  { id: 'metamorphic', label: 'Metamorphic rocks' },
  { id: 'processes', label: 'The processes' },
  { id: 'driving', label: 'What drives the cycle' },
  { id: 'identify', label: 'Identifying rocks' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Earle, S. (2019). Physical Geology (2nd ed.), Chapter 3: Intrusive Igneous Rocks; Chapter 6: Sediments and Sedimentary Rocks; Chapter 7: Metamorphism. BCcampus Open Education.', url: 'https://opentextbc.ca/physicalgeology2ed/' },
  { text: 'U.S. Geological Survey. Rocks and Minerals: The Rock Cycle.', url: 'https://www.usgs.gov/programs/mineral-resources-program' },
  { text: 'Grotzinger, J., & Jordan, T. H. (2020). Understanding Earth (8th ed.). W. H. Freeman.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="earth-science"
  topic="rock-cycle"
  description="Interactive rock cycle simulator: weather, bury, heat, melt, erupt and uplift rocks to turn granite into sand, sandstone, quartzite, magma and back again."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={rocksQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      The rocks around you are not permanent. Over millions of years, mountains wear down into sand, sand becomes stone,
      stone is baked and squeezed into new rock, and rock can melt and rise again as lava. Pick a rock and push it around
      the rock cycle yourself.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <RocksSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>starting rock</strong>.</li>
      <li>Press a <strong>process</strong> button. Watch the rock travel through the landscape and see the new rock on the right.</li>
      <li>Faded buttons are not possible right now; hover over or press one to find out why (for example, buried rock must be uplifted before it can weather).</li>
      <li>Follow your rock’s <strong>journey</strong> and the rough <strong>time</strong> it would take.</li>
      <li>Challenge: turn granite into marble. (Hint: granite contains no calcium carbonate, so you will need a few steps and some help from sea creatures.)</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is the rock cycle?</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>rock cycle</strong> describes how Earth’s rocks are continually changed from one type into another. There
    are three main types: <strong>igneous</strong>, <strong>sedimentary</strong> and <strong>metamorphic</strong>. No rock
    is created from nothing or destroyed: the same material is recycled again and again by processes on the surface and
    deep inside the Earth.
  </p>

  <h2 id="igneous">Igneous rocks</h2>
  <p>
    Igneous rocks form when <strong>magma</strong> (molten rock underground) or <strong>lava</strong> (molten rock at the
    surface) cools and crystallises. <strong>Intrusive</strong> igneous rocks such as granite and gabbro cool slowly
    underground and have large crystals you can see. <strong>Extrusive</strong> rocks such as basalt and rhyolite cool
    quickly at the surface, so their crystals are tiny; lava that cools almost instantly forms volcanic glass (obsidian).
    The ocean floor is made mostly of basalt, and the continents contain a lot of granite.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sedimentary">Sedimentary rocks</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Weathering</strong> breaks rocks into fragments and dissolved minerals; <strong>erosion</strong> carries them
    away by rivers, glaciers, wind and waves. When the sediment settles, usually in lakes and seas, it builds up in layers.
    Over time the weight of new layers <strong>compacts</strong> the older ones and minerals <strong>cement</strong> the
    grains together. Sandstone (from sand), shale (from mud) and limestone (largely from shells) are common examples.
    Sedimentary rocks cover about three quarters of the land surface and hold almost all fossils.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metamorphic">Metamorphic rocks</h2>
  <p>
    When rocks are buried deeply or caught between colliding plates, high temperature and pressure change their minerals
    and texture <strong>without melting</strong>. This is <strong>metamorphism</strong>. Limestone becomes marble,
    sandstone becomes quartzite, and shale becomes slate, then schist, then gneiss as conditions get more extreme.
    Pressure often lines minerals up in layers, a texture called <strong>foliation</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="processes">The processes</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>Changes</th><th>Typical time</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Weathering and erosion</td><td>Rock → sediment</td><td>Thousands to millions of years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Deposition, compaction, cementation</td><td>Sediment → sedimentary rock</td><td>Millions of years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heat and pressure</td><td>Any rock → metamorphic rock</td><td>Millions to tens of millions of years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Melting</td><td>Any rock → magma</td><td>Depends on depth and heat</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cooling and crystallisation</td><td>Magma → igneous rock</td><td>Days (lava) to a million years (large bodies of magma)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Uplift</td><td>Buried rock → surface</td><td>Millions of years</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="driving">What drives the cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Two engines power the rock cycle. <strong>Energy from the Sun</strong> drives the weather and water cycle that weather
    and erode rocks at the surface. <strong>Heat from Earth’s interior</strong> drives plate tectonics, which buries rocks,
    melts them, builds mountains and lifts deep rocks back to the surface. Without plate tectonics, Earth’s surface would
    gradually wear flat.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identify">Identifying rocks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Interlocking crystals</strong> with no layers: probably igneous. Large crystals mean slow cooling.</li>
    <li><strong>Grains, layers or fossils</strong>, sometimes crumbly: probably sedimentary. Limestone fizzes in dilute acid.</li>
    <li><strong>Bands, sheets or shiny aligned crystals</strong>: probably metamorphic.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Weathering / erosion</dt>
    <dd>Breaking rock down where it is / carrying the pieces away.</dd>
    <dt>Lithification</dt>
    <dd>Turning sediment into rock by compaction and cementation.</dd>
    <dt>Intrusive / extrusive</dt>
    <dd>Igneous rock that cooled underground / at the surface.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Rocks always go igneous → sedimentary → metamorphic in order.”</strong> There are many shortcuts in every direction.</li>
    <li><strong>“Metamorphic rocks are melted rocks.”</strong> Metamorphism happens in the solid state; melting makes magma.</li>
    <li><strong>“Rocks never change.”</strong> They change constantly, just very slowly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Igneous rocks come from cooling magma; sedimentary rocks from compacted sediment; metamorphic rocks from heat and pressure.</li>
    <li>Cooling speed controls crystal size in igneous rocks.</li>
    <li>Any rock type can become any other through different processes.</li>
    <li>The Sun’s energy and Earth’s internal heat keep the cycle going.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: real rocks follow many more paths and form mixtures; the times shown are rough orders of magnitude.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
